{"title":"Grid Jumps with Direction Lock","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"You are given a grid of `R` rows and `C` columns. Each cell is either free (`.`) or blocked (`#`). You start on a free **start** cell and want to reach a free **end** cell using as few **jumps** as possible.\n\nA jump goes in one of the four directions (up, down, left, right) along a row or column, over any positive number of cells `L` (the **jump length**). The rules are:\n\n- You must **land** on a free cell and stay inside the grid. You are allowed to fly **over** blocked cells; only the landing cell matters.\n- A jump of length `1` has no further effect on the next jump.\n- After a jump of length `L ≥ 2`, the **next jump must go in the same direction** as that jump (it can have any length, including 1). If that is impossible, the path ends there.\n- The first jump is unrestricted.\n\nPrint the minimum number of jumps needed to get from the start cell to the end cell, or `-1` if it cannot be done. If the start and end are the same cell the answer is `0`.\n\nRows are numbered from `0` at the top and columns from `0` on the left.","inputFormat":"The first line contains `R` and `C`.\nThe next `R` lines each contain a string of `C` characters, `.` or `#`.\nThe last line contains four integers `sr sc er ec`: the start cell and the end cell.","outputFormat":"Print a single integer: the minimum number of jumps, or `-1`.","constraints":["1 ≤ R, C ≤ 50","The start and end cells are free"],"examples":[{"input":"3 3\n...\n...\n...\n0 0 2 2","output":"3","explanation":"Without the direction rule two long jumps would do: (0,0) to (0,2) to (2,2). But the first jump has length 2, so the next jump would have to continue to the right, which leaves the grid. Instead: (0,0) to (0,1) with length 1 (no restriction), then (0,1) to (0,2) with length 1, then (0,2) to (2,2) down with length 2. Three jumps is the minimum: no route with two jumps exists."},{"input":"2 4\n.#..\n..#.\n0 0 1 3","output":"2","explanation":"From (0,0) jump down to (1,0), then jump right over the blocked cell (1,2) straight to the end (1,3). Two jumps; a single jump cannot reach the end because it is in a different row and column."}],"tests":[{"input":"1 1\n.\n0 0 0 0","output":"0"},{"input":"1 5\n.#...\n0 0 0 4","output":"1"},{"input":"2 2\n.#\n#.\n0 0 1 1","output":"-1"},{"input":"1 2\n..\n0 0 0 1","output":"1"},{"input":"4 6\n##..#.\n####.#\n#...#.\n..#...\n2 1 0 5","output":"4"},{"input":"5 5\n.....\n.....\n.....\n.....\n.....\n2 3 3 3","output":"1"},{"input":"4 2\n.#\n..\n..\n#.\n0 0 1 1","output":"2"},{"input":"5 4\n....\n....\n...#\n....\n....\n1 3 1 0","output":"1"},{"input":"4 3\n...\n##.\n.#.\n..#\n1 2 0 0","output":"2"},{"input":"2 3\n.#.\n...\n0 2 1 0","output":"2"},{"input":"2 3\n...\n...\n1 1 0 0","output":"2"},{"input":"4 3\n..#\n...\n.##\n#..\n0 0 1 0","output":"1"},{"input":"5 5\n..##.\n.#..#\n.....\n.....\n####.\n3 0 3 4","output":"1"},{"input":"2 1\n.\n.\n0 0 1 0","output":"1"},{"input":"5 4\n....\n..#.\n..#.\n....\n....\n3 1 0 1","output":"1"},{"input":"5 3\n.#.\n##.\n..#\n.#.\n.##\n3 2 4 0","output":"-1"},{"input":"3 2\n..\n..\n.#\n0 1 1 1","output":"1"},{"input":"5 3\n..#\n..#\n...\n...\n...\n2 0 3 1","output":"2"},{"input":"50 50\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..................................................\n..............................